Restore the GC protection that 1.16.0 removed from src/wdl.cpp.

CRAN spp 1.16.0 ships spp_lwcc() and spp_wtd() -- the two narrow peak-calling
kernels reached from find.binding.positions() -- with every PROTECT() call
removed and every UNPROTECT() commented out. The result vectors (nv, rpp_R,
rpv_R, names_R, ans_R) are therefore unprotected R allocations, so any later
allocVector() or mkChar() inside the same .Call can trigger the garbage
collector, which sees them as unreferenced and is free to reclaim or reuse the
cells while the C code is still writing to them and while the caller is still
holding the returned SEXP.

The failure is nondeterministic and depends on heap state, not on the input
data. When a reclaimed cell is re-allocated with a different type, R raises

    INTEGER() can only be applied to a 'integer', not a '<other type>'

from inside find.binding.positions(). When it is not, the run completes and
returns the reclaimed cells as peak positions and scores, so a job that does not
crash can still write silently wrong peaks. Both outcomes have been observed
against bioconda's r-spp 1.16.0 (build 12, R 4.4.3): 'not a character' and 'not
a double' from the same target on different days, and one target that failed
three times and then succeeded weeks later with nothing changed.
ENCODE-DCC/chip-seq-pipeline2#126 reports the identical message out of lwcc(),
the sibling kernel in this same file, with one pseudo-replicate failing while
its sibling succeeded; that issue does not state its spp version, so it is a
consistent report rather than a confirmed instance.

How 1.16.0 got this way, and why it cannot be fixed by a backport.

The removal is upstream's own work, on the hms-dbmi/spp branch that produced the
CRAN submission: commit 6c1f7cf "changing PROTECT to UNPROTECT in spp_lwcc() and
spp_wtd() functions" followed by ad0cc9c "deletion of UNPROTECT as it gave an
error", both 2019-05-23 on origin/cran-devel. src/wdl.cpp at the tip of that
branch (a09d16a) is byte-identical to the file in the CRAN 1.16.0 tarball.

The error that prompted the deletion was real. Upstream master (7f405c9, still
version 1.15.5) does carry live PROTECT/UNPROTECT calls, but its counts do not
balance: nv is protected once at its declaration and again inside the
!return_peaks branch, while the exits are UNPROTECT(4) and UNPROTECT(1), so
every call through either function leaks exactly one protection slot. Deleting
the calls silenced the resulting stack-imbalance complaint and reintroduced the
much worse problem it had been hiding.

So there is no upstream tree to backport from. master is behind CRAN by version
and unbalanced by protection count; cran-devel is the tree this patch fixes.
The patch instead balances protection per branch, which is what both earlier
states got wrong:

  * the return_peaks path protects rpp_R, rpv_R, names_R and ans_R, and exits
    UNPROTECT(4);
  * the !return_peaks path protects nv, and exits UNPROTECT(1);
  * nv is initialised to R_NilValue rather than to a throwaway allocVector().
    R_NilValue is what silences the -Wmaybe-uninitialized warning the
    declaration-site allocation was added for, and it needs no protection
    because it allocates nothing.

Dropping that declaration-site allocation is worth noting on its own. It runs
unconditionally in 1.16.0, but d_nv is only ever assigned inside
if(!return_peaks), so on the return_peaks path -- the only path
window.chr.call.mirror.binding() ever takes -- it allocates a REALSXP of
LENGTH(x_R) and abandons it. x_R is the strand histogram built by wtd(), of
length window.size + 2*whs + 1, and find.binding.positions() defaults
window.size to 4e7: roughly 320 MB allocated and immediately garbaged per call,
per window, per worker. Removing it lowers both peak memory and the allocation
pressure that decides when the collector runs.

The same code has been running in production as descostesn::r-spp 1.16.1
(github.com/descostesn/SPP-copy) since August 2026; this patch is that fork's
src/wdl.cpp verbatim.

Two deliberate limits on scope. The fork also bumps DESCRIPTION to 1.16.1; that
hunk is left out so packageVersion("spp") keeps reporting the version this
recipe declares -- hms-dbmi/spp#45 is a user asking which spp they actually have
after installing from bioconda, and a patched build that self-reports a version
CRAN never published would make that worse. And the MD5 hunk is only the one
line for src/wdl.cpp, so tools::checkMD5sums("spp") stays TRUE for the installed
package instead of failing on a file this recipe changed.

Not yet offered upstream: hms-dbmi/spp has not had a commit since 2020-09-18,
its last push was a contributor's branch in July 2021, and issues opened since
(#45 in December 2023, #46 in May 2024) have no maintainer response. PR #41,
which would merge the cran-devel tree into master, has been open since November
2020 -- so upstream master has never even received the code this patch repairs.
Happy to drop this patch if the maintainers would rather ship CRAN's source
unchanged.

